     These macros define data structures for and array-based red-black trees.
     They use a single, continuous chunk of memory, and are useful e.g., when
     the tree needs to be transferred between userspace and kernel.

     In the macro definitions, _T_Y_P_E is the name tag of a user defined struc‐
     ture that must contain a field of type _A_R_B___E_N_T_R_Y, named _E_N_T_R_Y_N_A_M_E.  The
     argument _H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E is the name tag of a user defined structure that must
     be declared using the AARRBB__HHEEAADD() macro.  The argument _N_A_M_E has to be a
     unique name prefix for every tree that is defined.

     The function prototypes are declared with AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE(), or
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__SSTTAATTIICC().  The function bodies are generated with
     AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE(), or AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__SSTTAATTIICC().  See the examples below for
     further explanation of how these macros are used.

     A red-black tree is a binary search tree with the node color as an extra
     attribute.  It fulfills a set of conditions:

           1.   Every search path from the root to a leaf consists of the same
                number of black nodes.

           2.   Each red node (except for the root) has a black parent.

           3.   Each leaf node is black.

     Every operation on a red-black tree is bounded as OO(_l_g _n).  The maximum
     height of a red-black tree is 22llgg(_n _+ _1).

     AARRBB__**() trees require entries to be allocated as an array, and uses array
     indices to link entries together.  The maximum number of AARRBB__**() tree en‐
     tries is therefore constrained by the minimum of array size and choice of
     signed integer data type used to store array indices.  Use
     AARRBB__AALLLLOOCCSSIIZZEE() to compute the size of memory chunk to allocate.

     A red-black tree is headed by a structure defined by the AARRBB__HHEEAADD()
     macro.  A structure is declared with either of the following:

           AARRBB<<88||1166||3322>>__HHEEAADD(_H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E, _T_Y_P_E) _h_e_a_d;

     where _H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E is the name of the structure to be defined, and struct
     _T_Y_P_E is the type of the elements to be inserted into the tree.

     The AARRBB__HHEEAADD() variant includes a suffix denoting the signed integer data
     type size (in bits) used to store array indices.  For example,
     AARRBB__HHEEAADD88() creates a red-black tree head strucutre with 8-bit signed ar‐
     ray indices capable of indexing up to 128 entries.

     The AARRBB__EENNTTRRYY() macro declares a structure that allows elements to be
     connected in the tree.  Similarly to the AARRBB<<88||1166||3322>>__HHEEAADD() macro, the
     AARRBB__EENNTTRRYY() variant includes a suffix denoting the signed integer data
     type size (in bits) used to store array indices.  Entries should use the
     same number of bits as the tree head structure they will be linked into.

     In order to use the functions that manipulate the tree structure, their
     prototypes need to be declared with the AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE() or
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__SSTTAATTIICC() macro, where _N_A_M_E is a unique identifier for this
     particular tree.  The _T_Y_P_E argument is the type of the structure that is
     being managed by the tree.  The _F_I_E_L_D argument is the name of the element
     defined by AARRBB__EENNTTRRYY().  Individual prototypes can be declared with
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__IINNSSEERRTT(), AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__IINNSSEERRTT__CCOOLLOORR(),
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__RREEMMOOVVEE(), AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__RREEMMOOVVEE__CCOOLLOORR(),
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__FFIINNDD(), AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__NNFFIINNDD(), AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__NNEEXXTT(),
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__PPRREEVV(), AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__MMIINNMMAAXX(), and
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__RREEIINNSSEERRTT() in case not all functions are required.  The in‐
     dividual prototype macros expect _N_A_M_E, _T_Y_P_E, and _A_T_T_R arguments.  The
     _A_T_T_R argument must be empty for global functions or _s_t_a_t_i_c for static
     functions.

     The function bodies are generated with the AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE() or
     AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__SSTTAATTIICC() macro.  These macros take the same arguments as the
     AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE() and AARRBB__PPRROOTTOOTTYYPPEE__SSTTAATTIICC() macros, but should be used
     only once.  As an alternative individual function bodies are generated
     with the AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__IINNSSEERRTT(), AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__IINNSSEERRTT__CCOOLLOORR(),
     AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__RREEMMOOVVEE(), AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__RREEMMOOVVEE__CCOOLLOORR(), AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__FFIINNDD(),
     AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__NNFFIINNDD(), AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__NNEEXXTT(), AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__PPRREEVV(),
     AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__MMIINNMMAAXX(), and AARRBB__GGEENNEERRAATTEE__RREEIINNSSEERRTT() macros.

     Finally, the _C_M_P argument is the name of a function used to compare tree
     nodes with each other.  The function takes two arguments of type _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _T_Y_P_E _*.  If the first argument is smaller than the second, the function
     returns a value smaller than zero.  If they are equal, the function re‐
     turns zero.  Otherwise, it should return a value greater than zero.  The
     compare function defines the order of the tree elements.

     The AARRBB__IINNIITT() macro initializes the tree referenced by _h_e_a_d, with the
     array length of _m_a_x_n_o_d_e_s.

     The red-black tree can also be initialized statically by using the
     AARRBB__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() macro:

           AARRBB<<88||1166||3322>>__HHEEAADD(_H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E, _T_Y_P_E) _h_e_a_d = AARRBB__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR(_&_h_e_a_d,
           _m_a_x_n_o_d_e_s);

     The AARRBB__IINNSSEERRTT() macro inserts the new element _e_l_m into the tree.

     The AARRBB__RREEMMOOVVEE() macro removes the element _e_l_m from the tree pointed by
     _h_e_a_d.

     The AARRBB__FFIINNDD() and AARRBB__NNFFIINNDD() macros can be used to find a particular
     element in the tree.

           struct TYPE find, *res;
           find.key = 30;
           res = ARB_FIND(NAME, head, &find);

     The AARRBB__RROOOOTT(), AARRBB__MMIINN(), AARRBB__MMAAXX(), AARRBB__NNEEXXTT(), and AARRBB__PPRREEVV() macros
     can be used to traverse the tree:

           for (np = ARB_MIN(NAME, &head); np != NULL; np = ARB_NEXT(NAME,
           &head, np))

     Or, for simplicity, one can use the AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH() or
     AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH__RREEVVEERRSSEE() macro:

           AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH(_n_p, _N_A_M_E, _h_e_a_d)

     The macros AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH__SSAAFFEE() and AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH__RREEVVEERRSSEE__SSAAFFEE() traverse the
     tree referenced by head in a forward or reverse direction respectively,
     assigning each element in turn to np.  However, unlike their unsafe coun‐
     terparts, they permit both the removal of np as well as freeing it from
     within the loop safely without interfering with the traversal.

     Both AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH__FFRROOMM() and AARRBB__FFOORREEAACCHH__RREEVVEERRSSEE__FFRROOMM() may be used to
     continue an interrupted traversal in a forward or reverse direction re‐
     spectively.  The head pointer is not required.  The pointer to the node
     from where to resume the traversal should be passed as their last argu‐
     ment, and will be overwritten to provide safe traversal.

     The AARRBB__EEMMPPTTYY() macro should be used to check whether a red-black tree is
     empty.

     Given that ARB trees have an intrinsic upper bound on the number of en‐
     tries, some ARB-specific additional macros are defined.  The AARRBB__FFUULLLL()
     macro returns a boolean indicating whether the current number of tree en‐
     tries equals the tree's maximum.  The AARRBB__CCUURRNNOODDEESS() and AARRBB__MMAAXXNNOODDEESS()
     macros return the current and maximum number of entries respectively.
     The AARRBB__GGEETTFFRREEEE() macro returns a pointer to the next free entry in the
     array of entries, ready to be linked into the tree.  The AARRBB__IINNSSEERRTT() re‐
     turns NULL if the element was inserted in the tree successfully, other‐
     wise they return a pointer to the element with the colliding key.

     Accordingly, AARRBB__RREEMMOOVVEE() returns the pointer to the removed element oth‐
     erwise they return NULL to indicate an error.

     The AARRBB__RREEIINNSSEERRTT() macro updates the position of the element _e_l_m in the
     tree.  This must be called if a member of a ttrreeee is modified in a way
     that affects comparison, such as by modifying a node's key.  This is a
     lower overhead alternative to removing the element and reinserting it
     again.

     The AARRBB__RREESSEETT__TTRREEEE() macro discards the tree topology.  It does not mod‐
     ify embedded object values or the free list.

